FB_PUBLIC_LOAD_DATA_ = [null,["Investigate a Suspected Intrusion on a Cloud Virtual Machine!",[[1369672112,"Analyst:",null,8,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[null,"\u003cb\u003eAnalyst:\u003c/b\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"]],[571066958,"Please enter your full name.",null,0,[[826458466,null,1]],null,null,null,null,null,null,[null,"Please enter your full name."]],[263281432,"Analyst Prep - Read Before You Begin","🔧 Technical Setup\nSave Your Progress: Log into your Gmail account to save your progress. This ensures your flag answers and investigation progress are saved as you work through the challenge.\n\nDocument Your Investigation:As you use Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (MDE) and Advanced Hunting:\nName your queries descriptively (e.g., \"Initial_Access_RDP_Analysis\", \"Malicious_Process_Detection\")\nSave timestamps of key events for your investigation timeline\nAdd notes to your queries explaining what you found - this builds your report as you go\nScreenshot important results showing IOC's in the logs\n\nTechnical Tips:\nCase-sensitive flag answers may fail if there are extra spaces\nCopy/paste carefully and double-check your submissions\nUse both MDE Advanced Hunting and Sentinel\nSave your working queries - you'll need them for your final report\n📚 Essential Resources (View These First)\nInvestigation Methodology:\n📖 SOC Investigation Guide - Step-by-step methodology for systematic investigation\n📊 SOC Investigation Report Template - Choose beginner or professional version\n🔍 SOC Analyst Hunting Starter Pack - KQL reference and hunting techniques\nReference Materials:\n🎯 MITRE ATT\u0026CK Framework - For mapping techniques and understanding attack progression.\n🎯 YouTube References: \nMyDFIR - Account Compromise\nTCM - Hunting 101\n\n🧠 Investigation Mindset\nThink Like an Analyst:\nFollow the attack progression systematically - Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → etc.\nDocument methodology, not just answers - How did you find it? What query worked?\nBuild a timeline as you investigate - When did each event happen?\nConnect the dots - How do events relate to each other?\n\nLearning Approach:\n\nUse the Investigation Guide to understand methodology before hunting for flags\nThis is investigation training, not just a flag hunt - Focus on learning the process\nDocument everything - Your report demonstrates professional competency\n🆘 When You Need Help\n\nIf You Get Stuck:\nTake a short break - Come back with fresh perspective\nResearch is part of the job - Use Google, documentation, and references\nReview the Investigation Guide - It teaches the methodology you need\nAsk in the Cyber Range forum - Community support available\nThink about the attack stage - What typically happens at this point?\n\nProblem-Solving Tips:\nCheck your query syntax - Small errors cause big problems\nVerify time ranges - Are you looking in the right time window?\nReview the scenario context - What attack stage are you investigating?\nTry different approaches - Multiple ways to find the same evidence\n\n🎯 Success Strategy\nComplete Learning Path:\nStep 1: Read the Investigation Guide to understand methodology\nStep 2: Work through each attack stage systematically\nStep 3: Document findings as you go (queries, IOC's, timeline)\nStep 4: Submit flags as you find them\nStep 5: Complete your professional investigation report\n\nFinal Reminders:\nEvery flag is there - The evidence exists in the logs\nTrust the systematic process - Follow the investigation methodology\nYou have all the tools you need - MDE, Sentinel, guides, and references\nThis builds real skills - Professional SOC investigation techniques\nDocument like a pro - Your report shows what you've learned\n\n✅ Pre-Investigation Checklist\n\nBefore starting the challenge:\nDownloaded Investigation Guide and Report Template\nReviewed MITRE ATT\u0026CK framework basics\nLogged into Gmail for progress saving\nHave MDE and Sentinel access ready\nUnderstand this is methodology learning, not just flag hunting\nReady to think systematically and document professionally\n\nRemember: You're learning to be an analyst, not just completing a puzzle. Every query you write, every IOC you find, and every connection you make builds real investigation skills. In your report you can add any other IOC's you find to have a more comprehensive report if you wish.\n\nYou can do this! Trust the process, follow the methodology, and document your journey. 🚀",8,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[null,"\u003cb\u003eAnalyst Prep - Read Before You Begin\u003c/b\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],[null,"\u003cp\u003e🔧 \u003cb\u003eTechnical Setup\u003c/b\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSave Your Progress:\u003c/strong\u003e Log into your Gmail account to save your progress. This ensures your flag answers and investigation progress are saved as you work through the challenge.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cb\u003eDocument Your Investigation:\u003c/b\u003eAs you use Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (MDE) and Advanced Hunting:\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003eName your queries descriptively\u003c/b\u003e (e.g., \"Initial_Access_RDP_Analysis\", \"Malicious_Process_Detection\")\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003eSave timestamps of key events\u003c/b\u003e for your investigation timeline\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003eAdd notes to your queries\u003c/b\u003e explaining what you found - this builds your report as you go\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003eScreenshot important results\u003c/b\u003e showing IOC's in the logs\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cb\u003eTechnical Tips:\u003c/b\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003eCase-sensitive flag answers may fail if there are extra spaces\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eCopy/paste carefully and double-check your submissions\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eUse both MDE Advanced Hunting and Sentinel\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eSave your working queries - you'll need them for your final report\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e📚 \u003cstrong\u003eEssential Resources\u003c/strong\u003e \u003cem\u003e(View These First)\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInvestigation Methodology:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e📖 \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.notion.so/CTF-Investigation-Guide-2b5cf57416ff8017a990c05340b3ab94?source\u003dcopy_link\"\u003eSOC Investigation Guide\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Step-by-step methodology for systematic investigation\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e📊 \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.notion.so/INCIDENT-RESPONSE-REPORT-2b0cf57416ff80d7b8fed7d2c6a6d764?source\u003dcopy_link\"\u003eSOC Investigation Report Template\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Choose beginner or professional version\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e🔍\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.notion.so/SOC-Analyst-Hunting-Starter-Pack-Beginner-Edition-272cf57416ff80edb3baef05cf7d9527?source\u003dcopy_link\"\u003e SOC Analyst Hunting Starter Pack\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - KQL reference and hunting techniques\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReference Materials:\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e🎯 \u003ca href\u003d\"https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0001/\"\u003eMITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK Framework\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - For mapping techniques and understanding attack progression.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e🎯 \u003c/strong\u003e\u003cb\u003eYouTube References: \u003cbr\u003e\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003e\u003cb\u003eMyDFIR - \u003c/b\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://youtu.be/RHmy5bGcKTI?si\u003d2viwTXlGdais34BD\"\u003eAccount Compromise\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003e\u003cb\u003eTCM - \u003ca href\u003